FACADE CRATE: {{ facade_crate_name }}
================================================================================
Generated by CodeBrix.RustTools.CrateBuilder (FacadeRenderer) from the mapping
plan of the Rust crate {{ package_name }} {{ version }}. Do not edit the files
in this folder by hand: CrateBuilder re-renders them, and generated-files.json
lists the files it owns (a re-render removes the ones it no longer writes and
nothing else).

WHAT THIS IS
  {{ package_name }} is a plain Rust library with no C interface. This crate is a
  cdylib named "{{ lib_name }}" (lib{{ lib_name }}.so / lib{{ lib_name }}.dylib /
  {{ lib_name }}.dll) that depends on the untouched upstream crate by path (as
  "upstream") and exports C functions for its public API. {{ header_file_name }} declares
  them for C; the generated .NET wrapper declares them for C#.

FILES
  Cargo.toml       the facade package (empty [workspace] table: this folder is
                   its own workspace root; panic = "unwind" in both profiles)
  Cargo.lock       the upstream workspace's lock file when it had one (cargo
                   adds the facade's own entry on the first build)
  src/lib.rs       the exports, the shared support code and the C mirrors of
                   the upstream types
  {{ header_file_name }}
                   the C header
{{~ if link_to_web_gpu ~}}
  build.rs         links the CodeBrix WebGPU engine (codebrix_wgpu)
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ for m in extra_modules ~}}
  src/{{ m }}.rs
                   copied from the WrapPlan's AdditionalFacadeFiles
{{~ end ~}}

C CONVENTIONS
  * Status: every export except the *_destroy and *_free_* functions returns
    int32_t: 0 ok, -1 null argument, -2 invalid argument, -3 upstream error,
    -4 panic, -5 object already used up. Out-parameters are written only on 0.
  * {{ prefix }}last_error_message(buf, cap, written): the calling thread's last
    message (UTF-8, no NUL); -2 with the needed length when cap is too small.
  * Strings in: UTF-8 pointer + byte length. Slices in: pointer + element count.
    A pointer may be NULL only when its length is 0. bool is 0 or 1.
  * {{ prefix }}buffer { data, len, cap } results: len and cap in BYTES (lists of
    numbers as native-endian bytes); release each exactly once with
    {{ prefix }}free_buffer / {{ prefix }}free_string. data is NULL when cap is 0.
  * Objects: opaque pointers from constructor functions, released with their
    *_destroy function (NULL ignored). A panic inside a call returns -4 and
    marks the objects of that call broken; a consuming method leaves the
    object empty (-5 on later use). Both can still be destroyed.

BUILD
  cargo build --release      (from this folder)

